          "children": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "{\n \"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n \"@type\": \"SoftwareSourceCode\",\n \"name\": \"Notation.lean\",\n \"url\": \"https://uuidna.com/lean/Notation.lean\",\n \"inLanguage\": \"lean4\",\n \"variableMeasured\": {\n  \"@type\": \"PropertyValue\",\n  \"name\": \"theorems\",\n  \"value\": 6,\n  \"unitText\": \"theorem\"\n }\n}"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page",
          "slug": "theorem-ten_reduces_to_one",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "tag": "h2",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "EVERY POWER OF TEN IS ONE, MOD NINE: 10, 100, 1000, 10000 all leave remainder 1. That is the entire mechanism of the digital root — a digit in any column contributes its own value and nothing more, so the digit sum carries the number's remainder."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "[10,100,1000,10000].all (fun p => p % 9 == 1)"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "theorem ten_reduces_to_one : [10,100,1000,10000].all (fun p => p % 9 == 1) := by decide"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page",
          "slug": "theorem-base_fixes_modulus",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "tag": "h2",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "THE MODULUS IS CHOSEN BY THE BASE. Three bases, three different rings, one construction."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "[8,10,16].all (fun b => b % (b - 1) == 1)"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "theorem base_fixes_modulus : [8,10,16].all (fun b => b % (b - 1) == 1) := by decide"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page",
          "slug": "theorem-bases_disagree_on_root",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "tag": "h2",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "THE SAME NUMBER HAS DIFFERENT ROOTS IN DIFFERENT BASES, and the disagreement is on this line: 432 leaves 0 mod 9 (the sealed harmonic marker) but 5 mod 7, the base-eight invariant. Nine divides 432; seven does not. A number is not harmonic — a number WRITTEN IN A BASE is."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "(432 % 9 = 0) ∧ (432 % 7 = 5) ∧ (432 % 9 ≠ 432 % 7)"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "theorem bases_disagree_on_root : (432 % 9 = 0) ∧ (432 % 7 = 5) ∧ (432 % 9 ≠ 432 % 7) := by decide"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page",
          "slug": "theorem-reversal_escapes_arithmetic",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "tag": "h2",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "DIGIT REVERSAL ACTS ON THE SPELLING. k432 fuses its two factorisations through rev(72) = 27, and 16 × 27 = 432 holds. It holds for that spelling alone: rev(75) = 57 gives 912 and rev(78) = 87 gives 1392, neither of them 432. The line proves the identity AND its two failures, so what was read as an involution over wings is shown to be a property of one written number."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "(16 * 27 = 432) ∧ (16 * 57 ≠ 432) ∧ (16 * 87 ≠ 432)"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "theorem reversal_escapes_arithmetic : (16 * 27 = 432) ∧ (16 * 57 ≠ 432) ∧ (16 * 87 ≠ 432) := by decide"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page",
          "slug": "theorem-root_survives_the_reading",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "tag": "h2",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "THE ARITHMETIC IS UNTOUCHED. Every digital-root fact the ledger seals stays exactly true — 432 % 9 = 0, and the nine units sum to 45 whose digits sum to 9. SCOPE: what this wing decides is that such facts are BASE-RELATIVE. The remainder is exact; the harmony read into it is notational, and the two are different claims."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "(432 % 9 = 0) ∧ (45 % 9 = 0) ∧ (4 + 5 = 9)"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "theorem root_survives_the_reading : (432 % 9 = 0) ∧ (45 % 9 = 0) ∧ (4 + 5 = 9) := by decide"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page",
          "slug": "theorem-nine_divides_by_construction",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "tag": "h2",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "AND WHY MULTIPLES OF NINE ARE NEVER EVIDENCE: every multiple of nine has digital root nine by construction, so finding one carries no information. Walked over the first eight multiples — 9, 18, 27, …, 72 — all leave remainder zero, every time, because that is what a multiple is."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "((List.range' 1 8).map (fun k => 9 * k)).all (fun n => n % 9 == 0)"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "theorem nine_divides_by_construction : ((List.range' 1 8).map (fun k => 9 * k)).all (fun n => n % 9 == 0) := by decide"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  }
}
